# Section 4, Element Types / Focal Planes: the NEW dialog for 3.2's focal_plane example (a Flat
# FocalPlane at z=2, axis +z, zElt 1d22), with NEW's echo of the element (printed before 'Is this correct?') in place of 3.2's typed "prescription
# data" block.  Dave 2026-10-19: Section 4's NEW tutorials become plain generated dialogs.  The answers are
# 3.2's.  3.2's element was the deck's fifth (its SHOW read iElt= 5); the setup (not printed) ends a
# far-field propagation there, as the text says focal planes do: a collimated 1.0 source at z=-1, a
# plano-hyperbolic lens (flat front at z=-0.4, n 1.5; back at z=-0.1, K -2.25, R -1.05: a perfect focus
# at z=2), a Flat Return at the focus and a Return sphere at the lens centered on it (FarField, 2.1) --
# the exit-pupil pair FEX uses.  Against 3.2's dialog: the type menus list more types; the dialog now asks
# 'Do you wish to use element-based coordinates?' for a FocalPlane too (3.2 did not; answered with its
# default NO); the source asks for base units (none) and the refractors for a glass type (default
# FixedIndex); the echo adds nCoat.  The OPD on the focal plane is at the round-off floor.
